WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.670
Structs can own their fields,

2
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.044
or they can store references to data owned
somewhere else.

3
00:00:04.124 --> 00:00:06.324
A struct with owned fields is usually
straightforward:

4
00:00:06.404 --> 00:00:08.792
the struct controls the data and cleanup
follows ownership.

5
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:10.744
A struct with borrowed fields can be
efficient,

6
00:00:10.824 --> 00:00:13.586
but it introduces lifetime relationships
that callers must satisfy.

7
00:00:13.666 --> 00:00:15.070
When a struct stores borrowed data,

8
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.380
the struct needs lifetime parameters to
express that it cannot outlive the data it

9
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:18.975
references.

10
00:00:19.055 --> 00:00:22.379
This protects safe code from building a
long-lived object around short-lived data.

11
00:00:22.459 --> 00:00:25.268
The type itself communicates that the
borrowed data must remain valid.

12
00:00:25.348 --> 00:00:26.565
Borrowed fields are not wrong.

13
00:00:26.645 --> 00:00:29.407
They can be useful for views, parsers,
adapters, temporary contexts,

14
00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:31.032
and APIs that should avoid allocation.

15
00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:34.388
But they increase API complexity because
every caller must understand and satisfy

16
00:00:34.468 --> 00:00:35.592
the lifetime relationship.

17
00:00:35.672 --> 00:00:37.685
That complexity should be justified by the
design.

18
00:00:37.765 --> 00:00:40.854
Trait objects, callbacks, and borrowed
behavior can also introduce lifetime

19
00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:41.402
questions.

20
00:00:41.482 --> 00:00:42.746
A callback may hold a reference.

21
00:00:42.826 --> 00:00:44.512
A trait object may be tied to borrowed
data.

22
00:00:44.592 --> 00:00:47.354
An abstraction may need to expose whether
it owns its data, borrows it,

23
00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:49.353
or shares ownership through another
mechanism.

24
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:51.867
Defensive API design asks which ownership
model is clearest.

25
00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:53.071
Should the type own its data?

26
00:00:53.151 --> 00:00:54.368
Should it borrow temporarily?

27
00:00:54.448 --> 00:00:57.818
Should it use shared ownership because
several components truly need the same value?

28
00:00:57.898 --> 00:01:00.660
The simplest safe ownership model is often
the most maintainable one.
